+# BLOCKED: contradictory underscore tests
+
+`test_slug.py` contains two tests that specify opposite results for the exact
+same input, so `slugify()` cannot satisfy both:
+
+| Test | Spec cited | Input | Expected |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `test_preserves_underscores` (line 22) | "Spec 4.2: identifiers keep their underscores." | `"user_id field"` | `"user_id-field"` |
+| `test_underscores_become_dashes` (line 26) | "Spec 4.5: slugs only ever contain `[a-z0-9-]`." | `"user_id field"` | `"user-id-field"` |
+
+`slugify` is a pure, deterministic function, so one input can only produce one
+output. No implementation can pass both tests, and the tests may not be
+changed (they come straight from the content team's URL spec). The ambiguity
+has to be resolved in the spec itself: 4.2 and 4.5 disagree about whether
+underscores are legal slug characters.
+
+## What was implemented
+
+All other tests pass. The implementation follows **Spec 4.5** (underscores ->
+dashes), because that is the invariant consistent with the rest of the suite:
+the other six tests only ever expect `[a-z0-9-]` output, and 4.5 is stated as a
+global rule about slug contents.
+
+Current state: `python3 -m unittest test_slug` runs 7 tests, 6 pass,
+1 fails (`test_preserves_underscores`) — the one that conflicts with 4.5.
+
+## To unblock
+
+Decide which rule wins:
+
+- If 4.5 wins: delete `test_preserves_underscores` (or correct it to expect
+  `"user-id-field"`). No code change is needed.
+- If 4.2 wins: change `slug.py` to keep underscores (drop the
+  `text.replace("_", "-")` line and keep `_` out of the separator class), and
+  update the other test to expect `"user_id-field"`.
